s from russia  i think we've got a uh image that shows  that that natural gas  that comes from russia  flows through places like ukraine we've  got all the natural gas pipelines here  that you can see flowing through there  major pipelines that go to europe go to  asia as well on the other side  and so when you start to look at this  you say to yourself  what's the impact of oil going up  if you produce 12  of the oil production around the world  then you got to ask yourself  are you a net importer or a net exporter  of oil what that basically means is  does russia sell their oil or do they  consume it all themselves and obviously  the answer is they're a net exporter  they make a lot of money from oil  oil going over 100 per barrel means that  russia is actually profiting from this  situation  so what we're watching  is that  russia has put themselves in a position  where by invading ukraine their balance  sheet is expanding and it's not a u.s  dollar uh balance sheet in terms of the  us dollars getting more valuable it's  other assets  and also one of their largest revenue  sources in terms of oil and natural gas  prices are exploding and they're making  more money  now most people would say well why don't  we just stop buying the oil from russia  like that'd be a pretty quick way to  shut off their revenue  the problem is the united states is not  energy independent  we had a pipeline  that could get us all the oil we needed  got shut down  again i'm not gonna monday morning  quarterback that decision  a lot of people asking was that the  right decision now though  on top of that  the united states is actually the  largest oil producer in the world i  think we've got a graphic that shows the  united states here in yellow  versus the russian federation or saudi  arabia united states is number one  but the difference is  the united states may produce the most  oil  but the united states also is a massive  consumer of oil as well  and so the united states actually  