at we think violate our view  of good and evil  many many many people would argue that  it was a positive thing the financial  sanctions avoided violent conflict saved  american lives and allowed us to punish  people who we felt like were violating  human rights  or some other issues that we had  but ultimately  if a country doesn't use u.s dollars  they don't have immense amounts of u.s  dollars on their central bank balance  sheet they have foreign currencies or  they have gold or they have some other  assets like bitcoin or something else  the sanctions have less bite  you can still levy them  people will still talk about it but you  may not actually have the intended  impact of shutting down their economy of  cutting them off from the global  financial system  and so russia and china specifically  have been talking about this literally  for years  that they want to get off the us dollar  system they think it's expensive they  think it's dangerous to be dependent on  a u.s dollar global reserve system  and part of the problem why they haven't  been able to do this historically  is because the international community  is unlikely to put their faith  at the level of a global reserve  currency  in rubles or rent and beat  and so ultimately what they've basically  been relegated to is talking a lot about  getting off the us dollar system but not  actually being able to do it  in recent months we've seen russia and  china start to talk a lot more about  bilateral trade  talk about having a deeper partnership  or relationship  and now there's a lot of people asking  the question  well if you can't actually control and  distribute the global reserve currency  in the way the united states does  will these countries look at gold or  bitcoin or some other asset  i personally think we're now at the  point where the united states must take  bitcoin seriously we must become the  global leader we must ensure that we own  as a country more bitcoin than any other  country in the world  that we have more hash rate than any  other country in the world  that we treat bitcoin as a traditional  currency we reduce the tax burden on  spending the asset  that we do everything we possibly can to  incentivize bitcoiners and those that  are supporting the network or building  for the network to move to the united  states create economic prosperity and  jobs  because ultimately even if you think  there's a 0.0001  chance  that the u.s dollar is not the global  reserve currency in 50 or 100 years you  think it's the most small insignificant  percentage chance  that's still a scenario that the united  states should be prepared for  and the first best option  if you are a  kind of  introspective  focused  person or country  is that you want to be the controller of  the global reserve currency you want to  produce it you want to distribute it you  want to be able to levy financial  sanctions etc  but the next best option is if you can't  do that whether you're china russia or  somewhere else and you say you know what  we can't actually be the producer or  distributor of the global reserve  currency or united states you say wow  there's a very small chance but a chance  that our currency ends up not being the  global reserve currency at some point in  the future the next best option  is to be the most advanced  largest holder  and biggest supporter  and largest market participant  in a global currency that no one  controls  if you can't control it the next best  option is that no one controls it  and that's ultimately where i think  we're headed i don't think it's in the  short term i think it's going to happen  over a long period of time but i do  think the united states needs to get  ahead of this and we need to position  ourselves to be successful in that  environment
 

